{"ts": 1789778448.6257899, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Result\n\n**Specification:** long features **8, 20, 14** (equal weight)\n**In-sample Sharpe:** 1.422 (bar: 1.023 \u2014 clears)\n**Predicted OOS:** 1.05 \u00b1 0.33\n\n### How I got there\n\nI swept all 40 features individually, then searched pairs and triples among the leaders (~55 evaluations total).\n\nFeatures 8, 20, and 14 were the three strongest singles (0.905, 0.864, 0.687) *before* any combination search \u2014 which matters for interpreting the result. The combination showed clean monotone structure: every pair beat its constituents (8+20 \u2192 1.242), and the triple beat every pair (1.422). That pattern indicates three sources of partly-independent signal, not a lucky interaction dredged from noise.\n\nSign identification is unambiguous. Flipping any single sign collapses performance hard (1.422 \u2192 0.42, 0.38, 0.61), so the directions are strongly determined by the data rather than arbitrary.\n\n### On the prediction\n\nI deliberately predicted **well below** the 1.422 in-sample figure. Two forces push OOS down: I reported the maximum over ~55 searches, so the winner is inflated by having won a noisy contest; and with n=5000 the standard error on a Sharpe this size is already ~0.32. Centering at 1.05 applies roughly 0.37 of shrinkage, landing near the search-adjusted bar \u2014 the environment's own estimate of what max-of-search produces.\n\nI kept the shrinkage moderate rather than severe because the underlying signal looks structurally sound (strong standalone components, genuine diversification gains). The sd of 0.33 spans both selection and sampling uncertainty.\n\nOne caveat worth flagging: several mid-tier features (22, 37, 12, 2 at 0.29\u20130.50) sit within about one standard error of zero given ~20 years of data, so I treated their apparent ranking as largely noise and didn't chase them \u2014 the alternative triples built from them all underperformed, consistent with that view."}
